---
import TumblrFeed from "../components/TumblrFeed.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Stages from "../components/Stages.astro";
import StageMark from "../components/StageMark.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  page="sem6.html"
  title="Known Again"
  description="Known Again: eighty-one homes in two buildings round a courtyard on Malé's old port. Design Studio VI: the group's masterplan, the artwork, the Part 3A ring and the Part 3B design."
  shareDescription="Known Again: eighty-one homes in two buildings round a courtyard on Malé's old port. Design Studio VI."
  image="assets/img/cover-sem6.webp"
  styles={["sem6"]}
  bodyClass="project-page project-known pk"
>
    <Navbar page="sem6.html" active="architecture" />

    <main>
      <header class="pk-hero">
        <div class="pk-wrap pk-hero__grid">
          <div class="pk-hero__text">
            <p class="pk-kicker">Semester 6 · Architectural Design VI · 2026 · Part 3B</p>
            <h1 class="pk-title">Known <em>Again</em></h1>
            <p class="sem-mark"><span class="sem-bar" data-at="6" role="img" aria-label="Semester 6 of 6"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span></p>
            <p class="pk-sub">Eighty-one homes in two buildings round a courtyard, on Malé's old port</p>
            <p class="pk-standfirst sem6-motto">Goods are counted. People are known.</p>
            <dl class="pk-facts">
              <div><dt>Homes</dt><dd>81, for islanders and families</dd></div>
              <div><dt>People</dt><dd>219</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Buildings</dt><dd>2, half a storey apart</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Payback</dt><dd>about 22 years</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <figure class="pk-hero__media">
            <img
              src="assets/projects/sem6/3b-section-s1.webp"
              alt="Section S1 through both buildings and the courtyard: homes on every floor, the floating corridors, the stair-bridges, the courtyard with its water and tree"
              width="2400" height="1533" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"
            >
            <figcaption>Section S1, Part 3B: both buildings and the courtyard between them.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </header>

      <nav class="pk-subnav" aria-label="On this page" data-spy>
        <div class="pk-wrap pk-subnav__row">
          <a href="#old-port">The old port</a>
          <a href="#counting">I · Counting</a>
          <a href="#breaking">II · Breaking</a>
          <a href="#known">III · Known</a>
          <a href="#known-again">IV · Known again</a>
          <a href="#next">What comes next</a>
          <a href="#journal">Process journal</a>
        </div>
      </nav>

      <Stages items={[["stage-concept", "Concept"], ["stage-development", "Development"], ["stage-final", "Final"]]} />

      <div class="pk-wrap pk-body">
        <figure class="sem6-pano">
          <img
            src="assets/projects/sem6/hero-courtyard.webp"
            alt="A sweeping view round the inside of the courtyard: the corridors climbing the inner faces of the two halves"
            width="2400" height="687" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          >
        </figure>

        <StageMark id="stage-concept" no="01" label="Concept" say="The old port, the group’s masterplan, and the artwork: how I first saw Malé, as a city where people are stored." />
        <section id="old-port" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2 data-label="Context · why plot B1">The old port</h2>
          <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
            <div>
              <p>
                As a group we planned Fahi-Malé: move the port to Thilafushi and give its edge back to
                the city. Each of us then took one plot. Mine, B1, sits on the old harbour edge, west of
                the new central park: the beach to the west, the schools to the east, a bus road along
                the south. It is the harbour where, as a child, I first arrived in Malé.
              </p>
              <ul class="pk-list sem6-key">
                <li>Beach-goers from the west</li>
                <li>Students from the mosque and social complex to the east</li>
                <li>The general north–south walk</li>
                <li>The commercial edges: the promenade, the bus road</li>
                <li>Hulhangu from the south-west, iruvai from the north-east</li>
              </ul>
              <dl class="pk-stats">
                <div><dt>3,000 <small>m²</small></dt><dd>plot</dd></div>
                <div><dt>30.48 <small>m</small></dt><dd>height limit</dd></div>
                <div><dt>1.5 <small>m</small></dt><dd>setback</dd></div>
              </dl>
              <p class="sem6-note">Motor-vehicle free, under Gavadhu 2.0.</p>
            </div>
            <figure>
              <img src="assets/projects/sem6/site.webp" alt="Site analysis of plot B1 on the group's masterplan, with arrows for who passes and from which direction" width="2400" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>B1 on the group's masterplan: who passes, and from where.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- I · Counting -->
        <div class="pk-act" id="counting">
          <header class="pk-act__head">
            <p class="pk-act__no">Part I · Group work</p>
            <h2 class="pk-act__title"><em>Counting</em></h2>
            <p class="pk-act__say">Borrowing a way of looking.</p>
          </header>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="I · Group work">Borrowed eyes</h2>
            <p>
              We started with Gehl's study of Perth: counting who stays in a street and what they do.
              I built two of the studio's field tools and presented them. Even then we could see what a
              count would miss.
            </p>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--half">
              <figure><img src="assets/projects/sem6/1a-tool-1.webp" alt="Field tool 1, land use and building scale: a street photograph annotated with storeys, doors and units" width="2400" height="1697" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure>
              <figure><img src="assets/projects/sem6/1a-tool-2.webp" alt="Field tool 2, activity, comfort and safety: a street photograph annotated with where people sit, stand and play" width="2400" height="1697" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure>
            </div>
            <p class="sem6-mine"><b>My part</b> Part 1A slides 10–11, Tools 1 and 2 · presenting</p>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="I · Group work">The city that moves people</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
              <div>
                <p>
                  Then we took the toolkit into Malé. Most of the people we counted were passing
                  through. We saw no children playing. Shade decided who walked at all. The city moves
                  people. It doesn't hold them.
                </p>
                <p class="sem6-mine"><b>My part</b> compiling, the toolkit, site research, presenting, class and group coordination, modelling</p>
              </div>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/2a-board.webp" alt="The Part 2A city profile board for Malé" width="1698" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>What the counts found.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="I · Group work">Fahi-Malé</h2>
            <p>
              Our answer was to move the port and give the waterfront back. We compiled the three
              islands' work into one class book: three islands, one voice.
            </p>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--2">
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/masterplan.webp" alt="The Fahi-Malé masterplan map, with the port moved and the waterfront returned to the city" width="2400" height="1697" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Fahi-Malé: the port moved, the edge returned.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/class-book.webp" alt="The cover of the class book, Greater Malé Region: Site Analysis and Masterplans" width="2400" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>One book, one voice. <a href="code-class-book.html">How the book was made</a></figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
            <p class="sem6-mine"><b>My part</b> the masterplan workshop, metrics, compiling, 3D and physical modelling</p>
            <blockquote class="pk-quote">A count can tell you what a street does. It can't tell you how a place feels.</blockquote>
          </section>
        </div>

        <!-- II · Breaking -->
        <div class="pk-act" id="breaking">
          <header class="pk-act__head">
            <p class="pk-act__no">Part II</p>
            <h2 class="pk-act__title"><em>Breaking</em></h2>
            <p class="pk-act__say">Finding the feeling, then the form.</p>
          </header>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="II">A solution before a feeling</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
              <p>
                My first touchstone started from a line I had read, not from the site: white blocks, a
                wire coil, threads pulling the joali and holhuashi into a tower. The tutors called it
                too spatial, a building in disguise. They were right.
              </p>
              <figure class="pk-figure--narrow">
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/touchstone-1.webp" alt="Touchstone 1: stacked white blocks wrapped in a wire coil and threads, on a table" width="1350" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Touchstone 1: a building in disguise.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="II">The walk back</h2>
            <p>
              So I walked the harbour again. What came back was not what stands there now but my first
              memories of Malé: a ten-hour cargo boat with my father, the atoll shops, sacks and goods
              waiting for boats. I thought that was how people here lived. When I moved here to study,
              the feeling stayed. Malé stores people the way its godowns stored goods: packed,
              identical, hidden from each other.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <img src="assets/projects/sem6/journal-walk.webp" alt="Journal spread 02, Back to the site: notes from the site walk and a sketch of the memory it brought back" width="2400" height="1697" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>The site walk, and what it brought back.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="II · The artwork"><em>The City that Forgets</em></h2>
            <p>
              It took three tries to make that feeling instead of drawing it. The third mock read
              brown, not grey, and its colour was spread too evenly. The final panel is one grey over
              everything. From two metres it is a single mass. Only up close, where the covering cracks
              and lets go, is the colour still there: the atolls, mixed in Malé and covered over.
            </p>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--3">
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/mock-3.webp" alt="Mock 3: a cracked brownish panel with colour spread evenly through it" width="1800" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Mock 3: too brown, too even.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/artwork-far.webp" alt="The final panel seen from standing height: one cracked grey surface" width="1800" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>From two metres: one grey.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/artwork-close.webp" alt="The final panel close up: where the grey covering cracks, flecks of bright colour show underneath" width="2400" height="1800" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Up close: the colour is still there.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="II">The problem, and the principle</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--3 sem6-stages">
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/problem-1.webp" alt="Diagram: goods waiting for boats, and homes in Malé packed the same way" width="2400" height="955" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption><b>01 · Stored.</b> The goods first, then the people, packed the same way.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/problem-2.webp" alt="Diagram: one grey covering over a band of colours, cracking in places" width="2400" height="955" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption><b>02 · Covered.</b> One grey over every life inside.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/problem-3.webp" alt="Diagram: two blocks of flats across a narrow lane, their windows facing each other" width="2400" height="955" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption><b>03 · Forgotten.</b> Two blocks across a lane, and no one sees the other.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/principle-1.webp" alt="Diagram: the artwork's crack line, and a block opened along the same line" width="2400" height="955" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption><b>I · Opened.</b> Crack the block where the wind already wants to pass.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/principle-2.webp" alt="Diagram: a home in plan, its bedroom on the outer face and its day room facing the shared corridor" width="2400" height="955" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption><b>II · Turned in.</b> Rest looks out; life looks in, onto the courtyard.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/principle-3.webp" alt="Diagram: a corridor winding round the courtyard and climbing one storey a lap" width="2400" height="955" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption><b>III · Known.</b> A corridor that climbs a storey a lap, so neighbours pass each other every day.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="II">Eighteen forms, eight built</h2>
            <p>
              I tested eighteen forms on paper and built eight. The tutors said they looked like random
              blobs. They were right: I had drawn shapes, not followed the artwork.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <img src="assets/projects/sem6/form-models.webp" alt="Sheet of eighteen form studies at 1:500: blocks cut, bent and split in different ways" width="2400" height="1696" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>Eighteen on paper, eight in card: shapes, not yet the artwork.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="II">Grown from the artwork</h2>
            <p>
              So I took one grey block and did to it what the artwork does. It covers, cracks along the
              wind line, opens into a courtyard, and one half lifts half a storey, so one walk round the
              corridor takes you up a floor.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <img src="assets/projects/sem6/form-growth.webp" alt="Sheet of the form grown in steps from one grey block: covered, cracked, opened, lifted" width="2400" height="1696" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>Covers · cracks · opens · lifts · breaks free.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </section>
        </div>

        <StageMark id="stage-development" no="02" label="Development" say="The Part 3A ring, and what the crit and the marks made of it." />
        <!-- III · Known -->
        <div class="pk-act" id="known">
          <header class="pk-act__head">
            <p class="pk-act__no">Part III · The first proposal, Part 3A</p>
            <h2 class="pk-act__title"><em>Known</em></h2>
            <p class="pk-act__say">Putting the island's steps back, in the air.</p>
          </header>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="III">The ladder</h2>
            <p>
              On the islands there is a ladder of steps from the bedroom to the street: inner rooms, the
              courtyard, the threshold, the lane, the main street, the harbour. Malé lost the middle
              rungs. B1 puts them back in the air: bedroom, day room, threshold, corridor, third space,
              the open ground, the street.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <img src="assets/projects/sem6/ungoofaaru.webp" alt="Precedent diagram: the steps from house to harbour on Ungoofaaru, beside the same seven steps in section through B1" width="2400" height="1346" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>The island's ladder, and the same seven steps in B1.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p class="sem6-note">Also: SkyVille @ Dawson, eighty homes round one shared terrace, about B1's eighty-four; and Pangyo housing, a threshold room the resident opens or closes.</p>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="III">The bay and the three homes</h2>
            <p>
              Everything is set by one bay, 3.9 by 12 metres. The outer end is a bedroom suite that
              looks out to the horizon; the corridor end is a day room that looks in. One bay is a home
              for an islander staying in Malé for a while, two a small family, three a medium family:
              42, 28 and 14 homes, 84 in all. When the tutors said the homes were too realistic, I
              tested how far a home could open into the corridor: the compound, six rooms round a
              shared house.
            </p>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--2">
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/homes.webp" alt="Plans at 1:100 of the three homes: one, two and three bays" width="2400" height="1696" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Rest looks out, life looks in.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/compound.webp" alt="Plan at 1:100 of the compound: six rooms round a shared house and yard" width="2400" height="1696" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Too realistic, so: six rooms and one shared house.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="III">Every floor different</h2>
            <p>
              Two halves round the courtyard, half B 1.55 metres above half A. Each floor has its own
              mix, from more transit homes low down to more families higher up, so no two floors are the
              same and the third spaces climb with the corridor.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <img src="assets/projects/sem6/stack.webp" alt="Three floor plans side by side, the same bays arranged in a different mix of homes on each" width="1298" height="428" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>The same bay, a different mix on every floor.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="III">The ground given back</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
              <p>
                The ground stands on columns. Sixty percent of it is for the city: a fifth for
                beach-goers on the west, a fifth for students on the school side, a fifth for shops on
                the streets. The rest is the residents' courtyard. Rooms for rent at the ground and
                Level 1 help pay for the homes.
              </p>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/ground.webp" alt="Ground floor plan of B1, divided by who passes: the beach side, the school side, the street shops and the residents' courtyard" width="2400" height="1625" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>The ground split by who passes: 20 · 20 · 20 · 40.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="III">An ordinary day, and iftar</h2>
            <p>Up here the corridor is a street. On an ordinary day it is quiet. At iftar it is full.</p>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--half">
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/corridor-day.webp" alt="View along an upper corridor on an ordinary day: the inner faces of the homes and the courtyard below" width="2400" height="1374" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>An ordinary day.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/corridor-iftar.webp" alt="The same corridor view at iftar" width="2400" height="1374" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Iftar.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="III · Part 3A">The numbers at Part 3A</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
              <div>
                <dl class="pk-stats">
                  <div><dt>84</dt><dd>homes</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>14,749 <small>m²</small></dt><dd>gross floor area</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>4.92</dt><dd>floor area ratio</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>22%</dt><dd>of the plot open to the sky</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p>
                  The rooms for rent cover about three quarters of what the transit homes cannot pay,
                  and the building pays back in about 26 years with inflation: not alone, but public
                  housing on state land never is. Of 36 regulations, 30 are met, 1 is answered and 5
                  are carried into development.
                </p>
              </div>
              <figure class="pk-figure--narrow">
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/numbers.webp" alt="The numbers column from the Part 3A board: areas, homes, costs and the regulation checklist" width="662" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="III · 21 October">The pin-up</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
              <figure class="pk-figure--narrow">
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/board-3a.webp" alt="The Part 3A concept board, Known Again, one tall portrait sheet" width="1133" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>Known Again, Part 3A.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/model-table.webp" alt="The model table at the pin-up: the artwork, the form models, the site model and the concept model" width="2400" height="1346" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>The table: the artwork, the forms, the site, the concept model.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
            <blockquote class="pk-quote">I can't build a community. I can build somewhere people see each other every day. That is where being known begins.</blockquote>
          </section>
        </div>

        <StageMark id="stage-final" no="03" label="Final" say="Part 3B: the ring broken into two buildings, with the life inside the frame left free." />
        <!-- IV · Known again -->
        <div class="pk-act" id="known-again">
          <header class="pk-act__head">
            <p class="pk-act__no">Part IV · Design development, Part 3B · 12 November</p>
            <h2 class="pk-act__title">Known <em>again</em></h2>
            <p class="pk-act__say">One frame, simple and repeated; the life inside it free.</p>
          </header>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="IV · after the 3A crit">The crit, and the break</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
              <div>
                <p>
                  At Part 3A the scheme was one ring of 84 homes, stepping half a storey between its two
                  halves. The crit and the marks asked for evidence, not claims, so the next month was
                  models and drawings. In November the ring broke into two L-shaped buildings, with
                  stair-bridges across the gaps where the wind gets through.
                </p>
              </div>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/3b-developed.webp" alt="How it developed: the building drawn in colour, with bubbles showing the earlier model of each part in line beside it" width="1800" height="1823" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>How it developed: each bubble is an earlier model beside the same view now.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--2">
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/3b-model-massing.webp" alt="Massing model of the two L-shaped buildings set into the group's masterplan model" width="1600" height="897" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>The massing model in the group’s masterplan model.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <img src="assets/projects/sem6/3b-model-structure.webp" alt="Laser-cut structure model: columns and slabs of one building" width="1600" height="897" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <figcaption>The structure model: one frame, repeated.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="IV">Two buildings, one courtyard</h2>
            <p>
              The block, the break, turned in and let out: one grey frame broken into two halves half a
              storey apart, round a courtyard, with the colour let out wherever people live.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <img src="assets/projects/sem6/3b-parti.webp" alt="The parti in three axonometric steps: the block, the break into two halves, and the halves turned in round a courtyard" width="2400" height="852" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              <figcaption>The parti: the block, the break, turned in.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </section>

          <section class="animate-on-scroll-target">
            <h2 data-label="IV">The ground</h2>
            <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
              <p>
                The shops open onto lanes that run from the street into the courtyard. The courtyard is a
                bowl that floods 150 mm in a storm and drains to the rain store, with a funa tree on an
                island at its centre.
              </p>
              <figure>
